.TH INSTALL 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
install \- copy files and set their mode, or create directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B install
.RI [ options ]
.I source dest
.br
.B install
.RI [ options ]
.IR source ...\&
.I directory
.br
.B install
.RI [ options ]
.BI "\-t " directory
.IR source ...\&
.br
.B install \-d
.RI [ options ]
.IR directory ...\&
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B install
copies files and sets the mode of each copy, or with
.B \-d
creates directories.
It is the
.BR busybox (1)
applet, which has fewer options than GNU
.BR install ;
see
.BR NOTES .
.SS Operands
With
.BR \-d ,
every operand is a directory to create.
With
.BR \-t ,
every operand is a
.I source
and is copied into
.IR directory .
Otherwise the last operand is
.IR dest ,
and at least one
.I source
must precede it.
With too few operands,
.B install
prints a usage message on standard error and exits with status 1.
.PP
When
.I dest
is an existing directory, each
.I source
is copied into it, named after the last component of the source, the
part after the last
.BR / ,
.BR \e ,
or
.BR : .
Otherwise
.I source
is copied to
.I dest
itself.
.B install
does not check that several sources have a directory to go into:
each copy then replaces the one before it, no error is reported, and
only the last source remains.
.PP
There is no option to copy a directory.
A
.I source
that is a directory is reported as omitted, and the exit status is 1.
.SS What is copied
A regular file is copied, and a symbolic link as a source is followed.
Only its data is copied, byte for byte.
The copy has the current time as its modification time unless
.B \-p
is given, and its read-only attribute is set from the mode, not from the
source; see
.BR \-m .
.PP
A source of
.B \-
is a file of that name, not standard input.
The device names listed in
.BR busybox (1)
are read as devices, so
.I /dev/null
gives an empty file and
.I /dev/stdin
copies standard input.
Such a copy keeps the current time even with
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-v
does not list it.
.SS Existing destinations
.B install
never writes into an existing destination file.
It creates the destination as a new file, and when the name is taken it
deletes the old file, even a read-only one, and creates it again.
So other hard links to the old file keep its contents, and a symbolic
link at the destination is replaced rather than followed.
A file that cannot be deleted, such as a running program, is not
replaced, and the copy fails with
.BR "File exists" ,
but a read-only one is left writable.
.PP
It is an error for a destination to be the same file as its source.
.B install
compares the volume and file index, so names that differ only in case,
names that reach the file by different routes, and hard links to one
file are all detected, and nothing is changed.
.SS Attributes
After copying each file,
.B install
runs
.B strip
if
.B \-s
is given, and then sets the mode.
The mode is always set, to 0755 unless
.B \-m
gives another.
So the copy is writable even when its source is read-only.
Directories made by
.B \-d
get the same mode.
.SS Output
.B install
reports each error on standard error as
.BI "install: " message
and goes on with the remaining operands.
The exit status is then 1.
Only
.B \-v
and
.B \-\-help
write to standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-pm444 ,
and may follow operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
The argument of a short option may be attached, as in
.BR \-m444 ,
or be the next argument.
A long option may be abbreviated to any unambiguous prefix, and takes
its argument after
.B =
or as the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.B \-c
Accepted and ignored.
Copying is the default.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-directory
Create each operand as a directory, with any missing parent
directories, instead of copying files.
A directory that exists is not an error.
Incompatible with
.B \-s
and
.BR \-t ,
and
.B \-D
has no effect with it.
.TP
.B \-D
Before copying, create the missing parent directories of
.IR dest .
With
.BR \-t ,
create
.I directory
and its missing parents instead.
Failure to create them is reported, and then the copy fails on its own.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-strip
After copying each file, run
.B strip \-p
on it.
.B strip
is found by searching
.BR PATH ,
and is not an applet.
It fails when
.B BB_UMASK
has the owner write bit set.
If it cannot be run, or exits with a nonzero status,
.B install
reports
.B install: strip
on standard error, followed by a reason when there is one, and the exit
status is 1.
The copy is kept, and the mode is still set.
Incompatible with
.BR \-d .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-preserve\-timestamps
Set the access and modification times of each copy to the source's
modification time, in whole seconds.
A failure to do so is reported, but does not change the exit status.
With
.BR \-s ,
.B strip
is asked to keep the times.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR \fIowner\fR, \fB\-\-owner\fR=\fIowner\fR
Accepted for compatibility, but ownership is never changed.
The
.I owner
must be a number, the name
.BR root ,
or the name of the current user, written with underscores in place of
spaces.
Any other name is reported as
.BR "unknown user" ,
and
.B install
exits with status 1 before it copies anything.
.TP
\fB\-g\fR \fIgroup\fR, \fB\-\-group\fR=\fIgroup\fR
Accepted for compatibility, but the group is never changed.
The
.I group
must be a number.
Any name, even
.BR root ,
is reported as
.BR "unknown group" ,
and
.B install
exits with status 1 before it copies anything.
.TP
\fB\-m\fR \fImode\fR, \fB\-\-mode\fR=\fImode\fR
Set the mode of each file or directory to
.I mode
instead of 0755.
The mode is an octal number from 0 to 7777, or a symbolic mode as in
.BR chmod (1)
that modifies 0755.
Only the owner write bit, 0200, has an effect: when it is clear the file
is made read-only, and otherwise writable.
A directory is never made read-only.
A mode that cannot be parsed is not reported, and leaves the file
writable.
A symbolic mode with no
.BR u ,
.BR g ,
.BR o ,
or
.B a
before its operator is limited by the umask; see
.BR BB_UMASK .
.TP
\fB\-t\fR \fIdirectory\fR, \fB\-\-target\-directory\fR=\fIdirectory\fR
Copy every operand into
.IR directory .
It is not created unless
.B \-D
is given.
Incompatible with
.BR \-d .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Write
.BI \(aq source "\(aq \-> \(aq" dest \(aq
on standard output for each file copied, and
.BI "created directory: \(aq" path \(aq
for each directory created by
.B \-d
or
.BR \-D ,
including each missing parent.
An intermediate directory is written with a trailing slash.
.TP
.B \-b
Accepted and ignored.
No backup is made.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument only, write a usage message on standard output
and exit with status 0.
The message omits the long options,
.BR \-b ,
and
.BR \-v .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when busybox starts, for a caller
such as
.B cmd.exe
that does not expand them.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for the programs it starts, so commands run from the kit's shell get the
shell's expansion only.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
An octal file mode creation mask, applied before
.B install
runs.
With the owner write bit,
.BR 0200 ,
set, each copy is created read-only until its mode is set, so
.B strip
cannot rewrite it and
.B \-s
fails.
The mask also limits a symbolic
.B \-m
mode with no
.BR u ,
.BR g ,
.BR o ,
or
.B a
before its operator, such as
.BR =rw :
it adds or removes only bits that the mask leaves clear.
With
.B 0200
set, for example,
.B \-m\ =rw
makes the file read-only, and
.B \-m\ \-w
leaves it writable.
.TP
.B PATH
Where
.B \-s
looks for
.BR strip .
An empty element stands for the current directory.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Messages written to a console also depend on
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and Windows error dialogs on
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
as for every busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line, with a usage message on standard error; an
unknown user or group; or any operand that could not be copied,
stripped, or given its mode.
.SH EXAMPLES
Install a program, creating the directory it goes in, and strip it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ install \-D \-s hello.exe out/bin/hello.exe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Create several directories:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ install \-d out/bin out/lib out/include
.EE
.RE
.PP
Install libraries into a directory, keeping their times and listing
each copy:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ install \-pv \-t out/lib libfoo.a libbar.a
\(aqlibfoo.a\(aq \-> \(aqout/lib/libfoo.a\(aq
\(aqlibbar.a\(aq \-> \(aqout/lib/libbar.a\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Install headers as read-only files:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ install \-m 444 foo.h bar.h out/include
.EE
.RE
.PP
Install a program into a Windows directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ install hello.exe c:/tools
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR install :
.IP \(bu 2
Ownership is never changed.
.B \-o
and
.B \-g
only check that their argument is acceptable.
.IP \(bu 2
The mode sets only the read-only attribute of a file.
A file is executable on Windows by its name or contents, not by its
mode; see
.BR busybox (1).
.IP \(bu 2
An existing destination is deleted and created again, rather than
overwritten in place, and no backup is made.
.IP \(bu 2
Several sources with a
.I dest
that is not a directory are not an error: each replaces the one before.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B \-m
mode that does not parse is not an error.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-v
is accepted although the usage message omits it.
.IP \(bu 2
Options not listed above, such as
.BR \-C ,
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-T ,
.BR \-Z ,
.BR \-\-backup ,
.BR \-\-compare ,
.BR \-\-suffix ,
and
.BR \-\-no\-target\-directory ,
are invalid.
.PP
Only a file's data, and with
.B \-p
its modification time, are copied.
Alternate data streams, such as the
.B Zone.Identifier
stream that marks downloaded files, other attributes, such as hidden,
and security descriptors are not: a new file or directory gets the
permissions it inherits from its parent directory.
.PP
Paths may have drive letters and backslashes.
Names in a directory are joined with
.BR / ,
so
.B install x \(aqc:\etools\(aq
installs to
.IR c:\etools/x .
A bare drive such as
.I c:
names the current directory of that drive, so
.B install x c:
installs to
.IR c:x .
Names may contain any Unicode character on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B install
expands wildcards in its arguments itself; see
.BR BB_GLOBBING .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR chmod (1),
.BR cp (1),
.BR mkdir (1)
